Company management review the carrying values of long-lived assets whenever events and circumstances, such as a significant decline in
the asset&#8217;s market value, obsolescence or physical damage affecting the asset, significant adverse changes in the assets use, deterioration
in the expected level of the assets performance, cash flows for maintaining the asset are higher than forecast, indicate that the net
book value of an asset may not be recovered through expected future cash flows from its use and eventual disposition. If the estimated
cash flows from the use of the asset and its eventual disposition are below the asset&#8217;s carrying value, then the asset is deemed
to be impaired and written down to its fair value.</span></p></ix:continuation>

<p style="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin: 0pt 0; text-align: justify"><span style="font-family: Times New Roman, Times, Serif; font-size: 10pt">&#160;</span></p>

